fa2aa0eb861306"},"seconds":29.365480661392212,"seed":168571000379642811,"stop":"eos","text":"We can use the properties of logarithms to simplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_2 y - \\log_2 3.\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nTo clear the fraction, multiply every term by 3:\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - 3 \\log_2 3 = \\log_2 y.\n\\]\n\nRearrange the equation to isolate \\(\\log_2 y\\):\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_2 y = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(y = \\log_2 x\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\). Since \\(y = \\log_2 x\\), we have:\n\\[\ny = 2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}}.\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}}\\), we use the property of exponents:\n\\[\n2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^{\\frac{3}{2}} = 3^{\\frac{3}{2}} = \\sqrt{27}.\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\n\\log_2 x = \\sqrt{27}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\):\n\\[\n(\\log_2 x)^2 = (\\sqrt{27})^2 = 27.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{27}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the problem, we start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's introduce a substitution to simplify the equation. Let:\n\\[\ny = \\log_2 x.\n\\]\n\nUsing this substitution, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to express \\(\\log_8 x\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Recall that:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{\\log_2 8}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_2 8 = 3\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} = \\frac{y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 x = \\frac{y}{3}\\) back into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to express \\(\\log_8 y\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Using the change of base formula, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{\\log_2 8} = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nWe can use the properties of logarithms to simplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_2 y - \\log_2 3.\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nTo clear the fraction, multiply every term by 3:\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - 3 \\log_2 3 = \\log_2 y.\n\\]\n\nRearrange the equation to isolate \\(\\log_2 y\\):\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_2 y = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(y = \\log_2 x\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\). Since \\(y = \\log_2 x\\), we have:\n\\[\ny = 2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}}.\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}}\\), we use the property of exponents:\n\\[\n2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^{\\frac{3}{2}} = 3^{\\frac{3}{2}} = \\sqrt{27}.\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\n\\log_2 x = \\sqrt{27}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\):\n\\[\n(\\log_2 x)^2 = (\\sqrt{27})^2 = 27.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}.\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:434"}
